  Now the other half of the sentence: what counts as a [1mchange[0m worth telling someone about. This matters more than it
  sounds, because a prototype that notifies on every keystroke teaches you that notifications are annoying — which is a
  real lesson, but probably not the one you're after.

  For reference, I'm assuming a minimal task: title, status, assignee, due date. That's the smallest model that makes
  the feature demonstrable.

  - [1mStatus changes only[0m — "Deploy script moved to Done." Highest signal, lowest volume. One event type, trivially
    legible. Risks feeling thin in a demo: you click, one line appears.
  - [1mStatus + assignment + due date[0m — the three things that change what someone else has to [3mdo[0m. Still a small, closed
    set, but three distinct notification sentences, which makes the feed look like a real feed. Title edits and typo
    fixes stay silent.
  - [1mEvery field edit[0m — anything that mutates a task fires. Most complete, and the fastest route to a noisy feed nobody
    reads. Useful mainly if the thing you want to learn is how bad noise gets.
  - [1mAbove, plus comments[0m — the biggest driver of real notification traffic in actual task apps. But comments don't exist
    in this app, so this means designing a second subsystem alongside the first.

  [1mMy recommendation: status + assignment + due date.[0m It's the smallest set where the feed reads like something real,
  each event type has a genuinely different sentence and urgency, and adding or removing a type later is a one-line
  change since they all share an event shape. I'd also default to [3mnot[0m notifying you about your own edits — you were
  there.
